#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088840} :activity/id #uuid "478b73f5-7a8c-56ae-b136-ca2543114338" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088952 :tutorial/content "Given any homogeneous first-order ordinary differential equation, we can always transform it into a separable equation through the substitution $y=vx,$ where $v=v(x)$ is a function of $x.$\n\nTo understand why, suppose we have a homogeneous first-order ordinary differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y).\n$$\n\nThen because $f$ is homogeneous of degree zero, we have\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nfor any $t.$ By letting $t=\\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =f(tx,ty) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x,\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =F\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the function $F$ is defined as $F(v) = f(1,v).$ \n\nNow, letting $v = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ (or equivalently, $y=vx$), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =F\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(vx) & =F(v) \\\\ v+x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =F(v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is now separable! We can separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =F(v)-v \\\\ \\frac{1}{F(v)-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe0a9e9e-ab23-5364-99bb-cddbfd67a4a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4107 :tutorial/title "Proof That Homogeneous First-Order Differential Equations Can Always Be Made Separable"} :step/id #uuid "e6e25f63-9efc-5f27-924c-23d2bc570eed" :step/math-academy-id 24848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088871 :tutorial/content "Consider the homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{x+y}{x} \\,.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be solved using separation of variables. So, how can we solve it?\n\nGiven any homogeneous differential equation, if we substitute $y=vx$ where $v=v(x)$ is a function of $x,$ then we can transform the differential equation into one that is separable.\n\nDifferentiating the substitution $y=vx,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the original differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+v \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential equation above is separable! We can solve it by integrating both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  substituting $y=v x$  we obtain the solution to the original differential equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =vx \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d2f64582-8688-5882-baa3-8712370f1568" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2061 :tutorial/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "cbc300bb-efe0-53f2-8cb2-54e525a87290" :step/math-academy-id 7887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087456 :question/id #uuid "34c357f1-505b-530f-8bb4-376eada248a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2301" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y = vx$  (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}    =   \\cos \\left( \\frac {y} {x} \\right)\n$$\n\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x.$" :question/worked-solution "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8edbecf-59d3-5f32-a0ac-d6e8f2128dc0" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526477} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650988}] :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "b109ba33-31dd-5507-9e3b-84e3db60d6e3" :step/math-academy-id 7889 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087887 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087457 :question/id #uuid "89b7276c-ef41-5123-ad31-b54753656111" :question/math-academy-id "e-2302" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](images/12/12aec388c94c8c4d4f2099b98f5a489d110fa3ef0d58e7895d9a425e49f8eb77.png)" :question/worked-solution "We note that this equation is a homogeneous first-order differential equation because\n![](images/c3/c3215663fd1a332f750b8af4049404061e2b1c64726c81572d1a1e76816bb718.png)\n\nThe given equation is not separable, but we can transform it into a separable equation by carrying out the substitution\n![](images/7c/7c57d5a7c4003269ba9eacf6240394f508347989a9d75665c7070e31cbdad701.png)\n\nCarrying out the substitution and separating variables, we have\n![](images/8a/8ad7a31876bc67fa69ff24ed41badcf6531ddf52b2d1847af4b0b75595ab5bcd.png)\n\nIntegrating both sides, we get\n![](images/7a/7a6577d6ac4f3f1fc100a2767d77509fbe211f7d3e7bc7a2bba027080e7e6ccd.png)\nwhere ![](images/42/4247e7a16afb46979bc6ecfc9ae10dc6c92d0f5f1ff5e1b2a1f412ec86a48a83.png) (with ![](images/9e/9e71f72815a80396d1186bfed1047662e1d507561c7160d1c2d89c2f23421c09.png)) and ![](images/01/012dd077bd0f68f82ae37fa50d493e97bd67ac5ff9f301e48ca29e91b1af16ab.png) \n\nFinally, substituting ![](images/f7/f74fcd854fc4be51cd8eed8f4d0649bcc7cf11089a7aa2a7e7edd8d306693f78.png) and simplifying, we get\n![](images/05/05eae130ba10a3386a1c8621ecc3e3d3ec0bb7af352fb4c928cf8bf2d88c21a3.png)\nwhere ![](images/89/8939a3ee49356a0cc8515cce98d84ecf10279dad9d2f76fac07660d0f99a0d7b.png) Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/18/185e5a72b055183d70fb6d9dbc0227190a8114b80b2dc4193d8f542d5c85c0d7.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49eaf082-07de-5468-a2d7-4cdfce27a553" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567187} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650967}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "40825fe1-131e-5189-a437-8a6a63c2fe77" :step/math-academy-id 7890 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088872 :tutorial/content "If we're given a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand the right-hand side is a **homogeneous function of degree zero**, meaning that it satisfies \n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = f(x,y),\n$$\n\nthen we say that the differential equation is a **homogeneous differential equation**.\n\nFor instance, let's take a look at the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{x^2-y^2}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is homogeneous. Here's why:\n\n- Both the numerator and denominator of the function on the right-hand side are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$\n\n- So, the rational function on the right-hand side is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2 = 0.$\n\n- Since the right-hand side is a homogeneous function of degree $0,$ the differential equation is homogeneous.\n\nBy contrast, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{x^3-y^3}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nis *not* a homogeneous differential equation. Although the function on the right-hand side is homogeneous, its degree is $1,$ not zero.\n\n**Watch out!** In this context, the word homogeneous refers only to the function $f(x,y)$ being a homogeneous function of degree zero. It does *not* mean the same thing as a homogeneous linear differential equation (such as one with zero forcing term). These are completely different uses of the word homogeneous, even though they share the same name.\n\nA differential equation can be homogeneous in this sense without being linear, and a linear differential equation can be homogeneous without being homogeneous in this sense. Always check which definition is being used." :tutorial/id #uuid "53343733-c3a7-588a-88f5-5c46d4dab2a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2106 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "949c13d7-21e9-51e6-96ae-ab65516cee5d" :step/math-academy-id 8087 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :question/id #uuid "f34de8cd-b650-5926-9f06-8127d4fb3a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-2393" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049922}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650949}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "327a0917-43f8-5e34-8186-3aef53fe0c2e" :step/math-academy-id 8088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088837}}] :activity/title "Homogeneous First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087943}]}
